@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Inputs}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_PasswordInput}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Usage}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Password_Input @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Usage</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Password_Input @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Usage</h1>

<p>
    The example below has the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">UpdateOnInput</span> parameter on both fields set to false, so validation occurs when focus is lost. The ValidationDisplayMode
    for both fields was set to <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">ValidationDisplayMode.DescribedByHintSuppressed</span> as I felt the validation message was sufficient to reduce verbosity
    for screen reader users.
</p>
<p>
    Optional icons were also added. All the input icons are hidden from screen reader users as they are just decorative. In the case of the state icons, green tick and red cross, screen reader
    users are informed via the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">aria-invalid</span> attribute..
</p>
<p>
    The "Show Password" element is a button so its accessible to all users, the normal "Eye" icon added by the browser, is not accessible to keyboard users.
</p>
<p>
    <b>Note: </b>I have used my open source Validation framework and its Blazor NuGet package <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Class" href="@GlobalValues.Validated_Blazor_Repo_Site" target="_blank">Validated.Blazor (opens in new window)</a>.
    but you can use any validators that work with Blazors <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">EditContext</span>.
</p>
<section class="@Section.Base @Section.Bordered @Section.Radius(SectionRadius.Content)">
    <h2 class="@Section.Heading.Base @Section.Heading.MobileCentred @Section.Heading.Text(SectionHeadingText.Secondary)">Password input example</h2>

    <EditForm Model="@_registrationData" OnValidSubmit="HandleSubmit">

        <BlazorValidated TEntity="UserRegistrationDto" BoxedValidators="_boxedValidators" AddDisplayName="true" />

        <div class="br-input-row">

            <PasswordInput class="br-col-xs-12 br-col-sm-6"  @bind-Value="_registrationData.Password" Required="true" LabelText="Password" ErrorsLabel="errors" HintText="Choose something only you know"
                            UpdateOnInput="false" PasswordAutoComplete="PasswordAutoComplete.NewPassword"  ValidationDisplayMode="ValidationDisplayMode.DescribedByHintSuppressed" SvgIcon="--svg-padlock-icon" />



            <PasswordInput class="br-col-xs-12 br-col-sm-6" @bind-Value="_registrationData.ConfirmPassword" Required="true" LabelText="Password Confirmation" ErrorsLabel="errors" HintText="Please confirm your password."
                        UpdateOnInput="false" PasswordAutoComplete="PasswordAutoComplete.NewPassword" ValidationDisplayMode="ValidationDisplayMode.DescribedByHintSuppressed" SvgIcon="--svg-key-icon" />
     </div>

    <div class="br-input-row">
            <button class="@Button.Base" type="submit">Fake Submit to trigger validation on unmodified fields</button>
    </div>

    </EditForm>
</section>

<ThemeBorderTool Title="Applicable theme border tools" ThemeToolHeadingLevel="ThemeToolHeadingLevel.H2">
    <BorderGroups>
        <BorderGroup Title="Input borders">
            <BorderRadius LabelText="Input radius" ResetText="Reset input radius" MaxRangeValue="@GlobalValues.Radius_Input_Max_Range_Value" CssProperties="@([new(CoreCssVariables.Group_Input_Radius_Base)])" />
        </BorderGroup>
        <BorderGroup Title="Button borders">
            <BorderRadius LabelText="Button radius" ResetText="Reset button radius" MaxRangeValue="@GlobalValues.Radius_Button_Max_Range_Value" CssProperties="@([new(CoreCssVariables.Group_Button_Radius)])" />
        </BorderGroup>
    </BorderGroups>
</ThemeBorderTool>
<h2>
    About the example
</h2>
<p>
    Please note that in general when using the Validated library the validators would be in a shared library so they could be used in any part of your application.
</p>

<CodeBox CodeBlock="@InputSnippets.Password_Code_Example" AriaLabel="Password input code example" />

<p style="padding-block:1rem;">
    For those interested here is the source code for the extension method that I made that works with my libraries BlazorValidationBuilder
</p>

<CodeBox CodeBlock="@InputSnippets.Validation_Extension_Method" AriaLabel="Extension method source code" />


